  "summary": "Public transcriptomic cohorts combined with WGCNA and multi-model machine learning yield candidate gene panels across many disease contexts.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is a repeatable workflow: public transcriptomes, both bulk and single-cell, are mined with WGCNA, protein-protein interaction or random walk with restart network ranking, and multi-model machine learning to prioritize hub genes, signatures, or biomarkers. Examples span neonatal hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, Alzheimer's disease, osteoarthritis, pulmonary hypertension, COPD, and preeclampsia, with one COPD study applying 12 ML models. Validation depth varies. The prostate cancer miR-7-5p/ACTC1 axis is tested experimentally, the Duchenne isonicotinylation model includes in vitro validation, and several studies rely on docking or deep learning to propose candidate compounds. What is newer is the layering of cell-type resolution onto cohort-level signatures, such as macrophage-associated and senescence-linked gene programs in pulmonary arterial hypertension and myeloid-cell LTA4H in knee osteoarthritis. Most outputs remain computational nominations that require prospective testing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
